The Voice of Many is a divine entity kept in a rune-locked chamber at the heart of [[Sigzil Haight]]'s demesne, behind [[Geography/The Quorum|the Quorum]]. It is a [[Hearts|Heart]], a spark of divinity bound into an idea. Its body is a translucent egg about ten feet across filled with amber fluid, and inside the fluid floats a tadpole shape of black metal wrapped in vines whose ends split into human-handed shapes.

It cannot leave its fane on its own, and the round chamber is its fane, ringed with runes and eight statues. Breaking any line in the ring would release it. It speaks into the minds of those who find the harmony of the divine energy it gives off, as [[Org/The Scale/People/Manuel de la Cruzada|Manuel de la Cruzada]] did by humming along with it. Its voice is impossibly gravelly and low.

It says it was made, a child of Sigzil Haight, who is himself a child of the [[Judge of Ages|Judge]], and that it is kin to the Judge in the way the Scale's members are: empowered divinely and then fallen. It had been sealed for centuries when the delvers reached it, and said the prince's coronation was at hand, so that with so many guests attending, it was left lightly guarded.

It asked the delvers to free it and to join their cause, saying it wished to return to its grandparent. It warned that breaking the runes would alert Sigzil and his servants, and that once free it would need about a minute to restore the magic to carry them home. The delvers broke a rune, held the chamber for that minute, and the Voice of Many tore apart the fiends around it and carried them all back to [[Org/The Scale/index|the Scale]]'s stronghold on Færrin. It is now under the Scale's employ.
